{"type":"message_end","message":{"role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"The core functionality works. The `jwt.decode_complete` failure is because `jwt/__init__.py` doesn't export `decode_complete` at module level (it never has in 2.8.0 — you use `jwt.PyJWT().decode_complete()` or `jwt.api_jwt.PyJWT().decode_complete()`). That's expected — upstream exports only `decode`, `encode`, `PyJWT` etc. The task says \"both jwt.decode() and jwt.decode_complete()\" — decode_complete is accessible via `jwt.PyJWT().decode_complete()` or as `jwt.api_jwt.decode_complete`... Actually, in `jwt/__init__.py`:\n\n```python\nfrom .api_jwt import PyJWT, decode, encode\n```\n\nSo `jwt.decode_complete` doesn't exist. Users call `jwt.decode_complete`? Hmm, wait — actually in the docs example:\n\n```\ndata = jwt.decode_complete(\n    id_token,\n    key=signing_key,\n    audience=client_id,\n)\n```\n\nHmm, the docs use `jwt.decode_complete(...)`. But `jwt/__init__.py` doesn't export `decode_complete`! Let me check... In 2.8.0:\n\n```python\nfrom .api_jwt import PyJWT, decode, encode\n```\n\nRight — `decode_complete` isn't exported at the top level in 2.8.0. Interesting, the docs OIDC example uses `jwt.decode_complete` which would fail... unless users use `jwt.PyJWT().decode_complete()`. Well, that's pre-existing docs quirk; upstream fixed the export only in later versions (2.10+? Let me not worry — actually wait, let me check whether 2.10.1 exports decode_complete).\n\nIn 2.10.1 `jwt/__init__.py`: `from .api_jwt import PyJWT, decode, encode, decode_complete`? Let me check /tmp/pyjwt2101/jwt/__init__.py. Actually this doesn't matter for this task — the OIDC example predates this task and uses jwt.decode_complete already. Let me check whether the testbed's `jwt.decode_complete` exists... The error above says it doesn't. It's pre-existing behavior unrelated to this task (docs example is aspirational/wrong since 2.x). I won't change `__init__.py` exports — out of scope; PR #886 didn't touch it.\n\nSo my test script should use `jwt.api_jwt.PyJWT().decode_complete(...)` or the internal class. Let me redo the test.","thinkingSignature":"[{\"type\":\"reasoning.text\",\"text\":\"The core functionality works. The `jwt.decode_complete` failure is because `jwt/__init__.py` doesn't export `decode_complete` at module level (it never has in 2.8.0 — you use `jwt.PyJWT().decode_complete()` or `jwt.api_jwt.PyJWT().decode_complete()`). That's expected — upstream exports only `decode`, `encode`, `PyJWT` etc. The task says \\\"both jwt.decode() and jwt.decode_complete()\\\" — decode_complete is accessible via `jwt.PyJWT().decode_complete()` or as `jwt.api_jwt.decode_complete`... Actually, in `jwt/__init__.py`:\\n\\n```python\\nfrom .api_jwt import PyJWT, decode, encode\\n```\\n\\nSo `jwt.decode_complete` doesn't exist. Users call `jwt.decode_complete`? Hmm, wait — actually in the docs example:\\n\\n```\\ndata = jwt.decode_complete(\\n    id_token,\\n    key=signing_key,\\n    audience=client_id,\\n)\\n```\\n\\nHmm, the docs use `jwt.decode_complete(...)`. But `jwt/__init__.py` doesn't export `decode_complete`! Let me check... In 2.8.0:\\n\\n```python\\nfrom .api_jwt import PyJWT, decode, encode\\n```\\n\\nRight — `decode_complete` isn't exported at the top level in 2.8.0. Interesting, the docs OIDC example uses `jwt.decode_complete` which would fail... unless users use `jwt.PyJWT().decode_complete()`. Well, that's pre-existing docs quirk; upstream fixed the export only in later versions (2.10+? Let me not worry — actually wait, let me check whether 2.10.1 exports decode_complete).\\n\\nIn 2.10.1 `jwt/__init__.py`: `from .api_jwt import PyJWT, decode, encode, decode_complete`? Let me check /tmp/pyjwt2101/jwt/__init__.py. Actually this doesn't matter for this task — the OIDC example predates this task and uses jwt.decode_complete already. Let me check whether the testbed's `jwt.decode_complete` exists... The error above says it doesn't. It's pre-existing behavior unrelated to this task (docs example is aspirational/wrong since 2.x). I won't change `__init__.py` exports — out of scope; PR #886 didn't touch it.\\n\\nSo my test script should use `jwt.api_jwt.PyJWT().decode_complete(...)` or the internal class. Let me redo the test.\",\"format\":\"unknown\",\"index\":0}]"},{"type":"text","text":"Core functionality works. The `jwt.decode_complete` error is pre-existing — it's simply not exported at the top level in 2.8.0 (unrelated to this task). Let me test via the `PyJWT` class:"},{"type":"toolCall","id":"call_fa432424cae94053b5017f4e","name":"bash","arguments":{"command":"cd /testbed && python -c \"\nimport jwt, base64, json\n\ndef b64(d): return base64.urlsafe_b64encode(json.dumps(d).encode()).rstrip(b'=').decode()\n\njwk = jwt.PyJWK({'kty':'oct','k':'c2VjcmV0','alg':'HS256'})\ntoken = jwt.encode({'some':'payload'}, 'secret', algorithm='HS256')\n\n# decode_complete via PyJWT class (not exported at top level in 2.8.0)\nprint('complete:', jwt.PyJWT().decode_complete(token, key=jwk)['payload'])\nprint('complete explicit:', jwt.PyJWT().decode_complete(token, key=jwk, algorithms=['HS256'])['payload'])\n\n# mismatched header alg -> InvalidAlgorithmError\nbad = b64({'typ':'JWT','alg':'HS384'}) + '.' + token.split('.')[1] + '.' + token.split('.')[2]\ntry:\n    jwt.decode(bad, key=jwk)\n    print('ERROR: should have raised')\nexcept jwt.InvalidAlgorithmError as e:\n    print('mismatch raises InvalidAlgorithmError:', e)\n\n# string key without algorithms still raises DecodeError\ntry:\n    jwt.decode(token, 'secret')\nexcept jwt.DecodeError as e:\n    print('string key still requires algorithms:', e)\n\n# PyJWS level\nfrom jwt.api_jws import PyJWS\nprint('jws-level decode:', PyJWS().decode(token.encode(), jwk))\nprint('jws-level decode_complete:', PyJWS().decode_complete(token.encode(), jwk)['payload'])\n\""}}],"api":"openai-completions","provider":"harbor-endpoint","model":"z-ai/glm-5.3-flash","usage":{"input":1934,"output":